  "body": "### What is the issue with the Compatibility Standard?\n\n(Written with assistance from GPT-6 Astra Max.)\n\nThe computed-value entries for [`-webkit-text-fill-color`](https://compat.spec.whatwg.org/#the-webkit-text-fill-color) and [`-webkit-text-stroke-color`](https://compat.spec.whatwg.org/#the-webkit-text-stroke-color) require an RGBA color. Taken literally, this resolves `currentcolor` on the parent before inheritance.\n\n```html\n<div style=\"color: red; -webkit-text-fill-color: currentcolor; -webkit-text-stroke-color: currentcolor\">\n  <span id=\"child\" style=\"color: blue\">Example</span>\n</div>\n<script>\n  const style = getComputedStyle(document.getElementById(\"child\"));\n  console.log(style.webkitTextFillColor);\n  console.log(style.webkitTextStrokeColor);\n</script>\n```\n\nChromium, Firefox, and WebKit return blue for both values: the inherited dependency resolves against the child's color. Resolving to RGBA on the parent would instead produce red.\n\nPlease align these computed-value definitions with [CSS Color's color-resolution stages](https://drafts.csswg.org/css-color-4/#resolving-color-values), retaining unresolved color dependencies until the appropriate later stage. The same distinction matters for color functions containing `currentcolor`.\n",
